/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Merge Intervals
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Sorting
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
Given an array of intervals, merge all overlapping intervals
and return a collection of non-overlapping intervals that
covers all the intervals from the input.

Approach:
First, sort all intervals by their starting time.

Then process the intervals from left to right while maintaining
the most recently merged interval.

If the current interval starts after the end of the last merged
interval, there is no overlap, so add it as a new interval.

Otherwise, the two intervals overlap. Extend the end of the
last merged interval to the maximum of both end values.

Why does this work?

After sorting, intervals are processed in increasing order of
their starting points. Therefore, if the current interval does
not overlap with the last merged interval, it cannot overlap
with any earlier merged interval either.

When intervals overlap, taking the maximum ending point keeps
the entire covered range and allows all consecutive overlapping
intervals to be merged together.

Algorithm:
1. Sort the intervals by their starting points.
2. Create an empty result vector.
3. Traverse every interval.
4. If there is no overlap with the last merged interval,
   add the current interval.
5. Otherwise, extend the last merged interval.
6. Return the merged intervals.

Time Complexity : O(n log n)

Reason:
Sorting the intervals takes O(n log n), while the merging
process takes O(n).

Space Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
The result may contain up to n non-overlapping intervals.
The auxiliary space used by the sorting algorithm may also
depend on the implementation.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> merge(vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {

        // Sort intervals by their starting points.
        sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end());

        vector<vector<int>> merged;

        for (const vector<int>& currentInterval : intervals) {

            // No overlap with the last merged interval.
            if (merged.empty() ||
                merged.back()[1] < currentInterval[0]) {

                merged.push_back(currentInterval);
            }
            else {

                // Merge overlapping intervals by extending
                // the ending point when necessary.
                merged.back()[1] = max(
                    merged.back()[1],
                    currentInterval[1]
                );
            }
        }

        return merged;
    }
};
